R99	Speaker Arg1:T77 Arg2:T78	
R100	Addressee Arg1:T74 Arg2:T75	
R101	Addressee Arg1:T74 Arg2:T78	
T79	Quotation 16356 16940	"My dear Harriet, you must not refine too much upon this charade.-- You will betray your feelings improperly, if you are too conscious and too quick, and appear to affix more meaning, or even quite all the meaning which may be affixed to it.  Do not be overpowered by such a little tribute of admiration.  If he had been anxious for secrecy, he would not have left the paper while I was by; but he rather pushed it towards me than towards you.  Do not let us be too solemn on the business.  He has encouragement enough to proceed, without our sighing out our souls over this charade."
T80	Entity 16334 16341	Harriet
R102	Addressee Arg1:T80 Arg2:T79	
R103	Speaker Arg1:T77 Arg2:T79	
T81	Quotation 16941 17014	"Oh! no -- I hope I shall not be ridiculous about it.  Do as you please."
R104	Speaker Arg1:T80 Arg2:T81	
R105	Addressee Arg1:T77 Arg2:T81	
T82	Quotation 17128 17203	"Well, my dears, how does your book go on?-- Have you got any thing fresh?"
T83	Entity 17015 17028	Mr. Woodhouse
R106	Speaker Arg1:T83 Arg2:T82	
R107	Addressee Arg1:T80 Arg2:T82	
R108	Addressee Arg1:T77 Arg2:T82	
T84	Quotation 17204 17425	"Yes, papa; we have something to read you, something quite fresh.  A piece of paper was found on the table this morning-- (dropt, we suppose, by a fairy)-- containing a very pretty charade, and we have just copied it in."
R109	Addressee Arg1:T83 Arg2:T84	
R110	Speaker Arg1:T77 Arg2:T84	
T85	Quotation 17699 17934	"Aye, that's very just, indeed, that's very properly said.  Very true. 'Woman, lovely woman.' It is such a pretty charade, my dear, that I can easily guess what fairy brought it.-- Nobody could have written so prettily, but you, Emma."
R111	Speaker Arg1:T83 Arg2:T85	
T86	Entity 17935 17939	Emma
R112	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T85	
T87	Quotation 18026 18613	"Ah! it is no difficulty to see who you take after! Your dear mother was so clever at all those things! If I had but her memory! But I can remember nothing;-- not even that particular riddle which you have heard me mention; I can only recollect the first stanza; and there are several.  Kitty, a fair but frozen maid, Kindled a flame I yet deplore, The hood-wink'd boy I called to aid, Though of his near approach afraid, So fatal to my suit before.  And that is all that I can recollect of it-- but it is very clever all the way through.  But I think, my dear, you said you had got it."
R113	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T87	
R114	Speaker Arg1:T83 Arg2:T87	
T88	Quotation 18614 18734	"Yes, papa, it is written out in our second page.  We copied it from the Elegant Extracts.  It was Garrick's, you know."
R115	Speaker Arg1:T86 Arg2:T88	
R116	Addressee Arg1:T83 Arg2:T88	
T89	Quotation 18735 19079	"Aye, very true. -- I wish I could recollect more of it.  Kitty, a fair but frozen maid.  The name makes me think of poor Isabella; for she was very near being christened Catherine after her grandmama.  I hope we shall have her here next week.  Have you thought, my dear, where you shall put her-- and what room there will be for the children?"
R117	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T89	
R118	Speaker Arg1:T83 Arg2:T89	
T90	Quotation 19080 19260	"Oh! yes-- she will have her own room, of course; the room she always has;-- and there is the nursery for the children,-- just as usual, you know.  Why should there be any change?"
R119	Speaker Arg1:T86 Arg2:T90	
R120	Addressee Arg1:T83 Arg2:T90	
T91	Quotation 19261 19569	"I do not know, my dear-- but it is so long since she was here!-- not since last Easter, and then only for a few days.-- Mr. John Knightley's being a lawyer is very inconvenient.-- Poor Isabella!-- she is sadly taken away from us all!-- and how sorry she will be when she comes, not to see Miss Taylor here!"
R121	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T91	
R122	Speaker Arg1:T83 Arg2:T91	
T92	Quotation 19570 19614	"She will not be surprized, papa, at least."
R123	Speaker Arg1:T86 Arg2:T92	
T93	Entity 18016 18018	he
R124	Addressee Arg1:T93 Arg2:T92	
T94	Quotation 19615 19725	"I do not know, my dear.  I am sure I was very much surprized when I first heard she was going to be married."
R125	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T94	
R126	Speaker Arg1:T93 Arg2:T94	
T95	Quotation 19726 19800	"We must ask Mr. and Mrs. Weston to dine with us, while Isabella is here."
R127	Speaker Arg1:T86 Arg2:T95	
R128	Addressee Arg1:T93 Arg2:T95	
T96	Quotation 19801 19943	"Yes, my dear, if there is time.-- But-- (in a very depressed tone)-- she is coming for only one week.  There will not be time for any thing."
R129	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T96	
R130	Speaker Arg1:T93 Arg2:T96	
T97	Quotation 19944 20383	"It is unfortunate that they cannot stay longer-- but it seems a case of necessity.  Mr. John Knightley must be in town again on the 28th, and we ought to be thankful, papa, that we are to have the whole of the time they can give to the country, that two or three days are not to be taken out for the Abbey.  Mr. Knightley promises to give up his claim this Christmas-- though you know it is longer since they were with him, than with us."
R131	Speaker Arg1:T86 Arg2:T97	
R132	Addressee Arg1:T93 Arg2:T97	
T98	Quotation 20384 20480	"It would be very hard, indeed, my dear, if poor Isabella were to be anywhere but at Hartfield."
R133	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T98	
T99	Entity 20481 20494	Mr. Woodhouse
R134	Speaker Arg1:T99 Arg2:T98	
T100	Entity 20607 20609	He
T101	Cue 20646 20650	said
T102	Quotation 20652 20855	"But I do not see why poor Isabella should be obliged to go back so soon, though he does.  I think, Emma, I shall try and persuade her to stay longer with us.  She and the children might stay very well."
R135	Speaker Arg1:T100 Arg2:T102	
R136	Cueing Arg1:T101 Arg2:T102	
R137	Addressee Arg1:T86 Arg2:T102	
T103	Quotation 20856 21005	"Ah! papa-- that is what you never have been able to accomplish, and I do not think you ever will.  Isabella cannot bear to stay behind her husband."
R138	Addressee Arg1:T100 Arg2:T103	
T104	Entity 21121 21125	Emma
R139	Speaker Arg1:T104 Arg2:T103	
T105	Quotation 21280 21539	"Harriet must give us as much of her company as she can while my brother and sister are here.  I am sure she will be pleased with the children.  We are very proud of the children, are not we, papa? I wonder which she will think the handsomest, Henry or John?"
R140	Speaker Arg1:T104 Arg2:T105	
T106	Entity 21065 21078	Mr. Woodhouse
R141	Addressee Arg1:T106 Arg2:T105	
T107	Quotation 21540 21674	"Aye, I wonder which she will.  Poor little dears, how glad they will be to come.  They are very fond of being at Hartfield, Harriet."
R142	Speaker Arg1:T106 Arg2:T107	
R143	Addressee Arg1:T104 Arg2:T107	
R144	Addressee Arg1:T80 Arg2:T107	
T108	Quotation 21675 21739	"I dare say they are, sir.  I am sure I do not know who is not."
R145	Speaker Arg1:T80 Arg2:T108	
R146	Addressee Arg1:T106 Arg2:T108	
T109	Quotation 21740 22396	"Henry is a fine boy, but John is very like his mama.  Henry is the eldest, he was named after me, not after his father.  John, the second, is named after his father.  Some people are surprized, I believe, that the eldest was not, but Isabella would have him called Henry, which I thought very pretty of her.  And he is a very clever boy, indeed.  They are all remarkably clever; and they have so many pretty ways.  They will come and stand by my chair, and say, 'Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?' and once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were only made for grandpapas.  I think their father is too rough with them very often."
R147	Speaker Arg1:T106 Arg2:T109	
R148	Addressee Arg1:T104 Arg2:T109	
R149	Addressee Arg1:T80 Arg2:T109	
