R282	Addressee Arg1:T218 Arg2:T215	
T219	Quotation 46670 46716	"Not near so often, my dear, as I could wish."
T220	Entity 46643 46656	Mr. Woodhouse
R285	Speaker Arg1:T220 Arg2:T219	
R286	Addressee Arg1:T217 Arg2:T219	
T221	Quotation 46717 47428	"Oh! papa, we have missed seeing them but one entire day since they married.  Either in the morning or evening of every day, excepting one, have we seen either Mr. Weston or Mrs. Weston, and generally both, either at Randalls or here-- and as you may suppose, Isabella, most frequently here.  They are very, very kind in their visits.  Mr. Weston is really as kind as herself.  Papa, if you speak in that melancholy way, you will be giving Isabella a false idea of us all.  Every body must be aware that Miss Taylor must be missed, but every body ought also to be assured that Mr. and Mrs. Weston do really prevent our missing her by any means to the extent we ourselves anticipated-- which is the exact truth."
R287	Addressee Arg1:T220 Arg2:T221	
R288	Speaker Arg1:T210 Arg2:T221	
T222	Quotation 47429 47452	"Just as it should be,"
T223	Cue 47453 47457	said
T224	Entity 47458 47476	Mr. John Knightley
T225	Quotation 47478 47837	"and just as I hoped it was from your letters.  Her wish of shewing you attention could not be doubted, and his being a disengaged and social man makes it all easy.  I have been always telling you, my love, that I had no idea of the change being so very material to Hartfield as you apprehended; and now you have Emma's account, I hope you will be satisfied."
R289	Cueing Arg1:T223 Arg2:T222	
