R339	Speaker Arg1:T256 Arg2:T254	
R340	Addressee Arg1:T250 Arg2:T254	
T257	Quotation 50816 51211	"I have no doubt of his being a most amiable young man. But how sad it is that he should not live at home with his father! There is something so shocking in a child's being taken away from his parents and natural home!  I never could comprehend how Mr. Weston could part with him.  To give up one's child!  I really never could think well of any body who proposed such a thing to any body else."
R341	Addressee Arg1:T256 Arg2:T257	
R342	Speaker Arg1:T250 Arg2:T257	
T258	Quotation 51212 51268	"Nobody ever did think well of the Churchills, I fancy,"
T259	Cue 51269 51277	observed
T260	Entity 51278 51296	Mr. John Knightley
R343	Cueing Arg1:T259 Arg2:T258	
R344	Speaker Arg1:T260 Arg2:T258	
R345	Addressee Arg1:T250 Arg2:T258	
T261	Quotation 51306 51812	"But you need not imagine Mr. Weston to have felt what you would feel in giving up Henry or John.  Mr. Weston is rather an easy, cheerful-tempered man, than a man of strong feelings; he takes things as he finds them, and makes enjoyment of them somehow or other, depending, I suspect, much more upon what is called society for his comforts, that is, upon the power of eating and drinking, and playing whist with his neighbours five times a week, than upon family affection, or any thing that home affords."
T262	Entity 51813 51817	Emma
R346	Speaker Arg1:T262 Arg2:T261	
R347	Addressee Arg1:T260 Arg2:T261	
T263	Entity 53658 53661	she
T264	Cue 53677 53683	saying
T265	Entity 53688 53690	he
T266	Quotation 53714 53916	"What a comfort it is, that we think alike about our nephews and nieces.  As to men and women, our opinions are sometimes very different; but with regard to these children, I observe we never disagree."
R348	Speaker Arg1:T263 Arg2:T266	
R349	Cueing Arg1:T264 Arg2:T266	
R350	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T266	
T267	Quotation 53917 54142	"If you were as much guided by nature in your estimate of men and women, and as little under the power of fancy and whim in your dealings with them, as you are where these children are concerned, we might always think alike."
R351	Addressee Arg1:T263 Arg2:T267	
R352	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T267	
T268	Quotation 54143 54221	"To be sure-- our discordancies must always arise from my being in the wrong."
R353	Speaker Arg1:T263 Arg2:T268	
R354	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T268	
T269	Quotation 54222 54228	"Yes,"
T270	Cue 54229 54233	said
T271	Entity 54234 54236	he
T272	Quotation 54248 54311	"and reason good.  I was sixteen years old when you were born."
R355	Cueing Arg1:T270 Arg2:T269	
R356	Cueing Arg1:T270 Arg2:T272	
R357	Speaker Arg1:T271 Arg2:T269	
R358	Speaker Arg1:T271 Arg2:T272	
R359	Addressee Arg1:T263 Arg2:T269	
R360	Addressee Arg1:T263 Arg2:T272	
T273	Quotation 54312 54341	"A material difference then,"
T274	Entity 54342 54345	she
T275	Cue 54346 54353	replied
T276	Quotation 54356 54529	"and no doubt you were much my superior in judgment at that period of our lives; but does not the lapse of one-and-twenty years bring our understandings a good deal nearer?"
R361	Speaker Arg1:T274 Arg2:T273	
R362	Speaker Arg1:T274 Arg2:T276	
R363	Cueing Arg1:T275 Arg2:T273	
R364	Cueing Arg1:T275 Arg2:T276	
R365	Addressee Arg1:T271 Arg2:T273	
R366	Addressee Arg1:T271 Arg2:T276	
T277	Quotation 54530 54558	"Yes -- a good deal nearer."
R367	Speaker Arg1:T271 Arg2:T277	
R368	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T277	
T278	Quotation 54559 54648	"But still, not near enough to give me a chance of being right, if we think differently."
R369	Speaker Arg1:T274 Arg2:T278	
R370	Addressee Arg1:T271 Arg2:T278	
T279	Quotation 54649 54999	"I have still the advantage of you by sixteen years' experience, and by not being a pretty young woman and a spoiled child.  Come, my dear Emma, let us be friends, and say no more about it.  Tell your aunt, little Emma, that she ought to set you a better example than to be renewing old grievances, and that if she were not wrong before, she is now."
R371	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T279	
R372	Speaker Arg1:T271 Arg2:T279	
T280	Quotation 55000 55014	"That's true,"
T281	Entity 55015 55018	she
T282	Cue 55019 55024	cried
T283	Quotation 55027 55412	"very true.  Little Emma, grow up a better woman than your aunt.  Be infinitely cleverer and not half so conceited.  Now, Mr. Knightley, a word or two more, and I have done. As far as good intentions went, we were both right, and I must say that no effects on my side of the argument have yet proved wrong.  I only want to know that Mr. Martin is not very, very bitterly disappointed."
R373	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T280	
R374	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T283	
R375	Cueing Arg1:T282 Arg2:T280	
R376	Cueing Arg1:T282 Arg2:T283	
T284	Quotation 55413 55439	"A man cannot be more so,"
T285	Entity 55444 55447	his
R377	Addressee Arg1:T285 Arg2:T280	
R378	Addressee Arg1:T285 Arg2:T283	
R379	Speaker Arg1:T285 Arg2:T284	
R380	Addressee Arg1:T281 Arg2:T284	
T286	Quotation 55468 55528	"Ah!-- Indeed I am very sorry.-- Come, shake hands with me."
R381	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T286	
R382	Addressee Arg1:T285 Arg2:T286	
T287	Quotation 55627 55649	"How d'ye do, George?"
T288	Quotation 55654 55674	"John, how are you?"
T289	Entity 55587 55601	John Knightley
R383	Speaker Arg1:T289 Arg2:T287	
R384	Addressee Arg1:T289 Arg2:T288	
R385	Addressee Arg1:T285 Arg2:T287	
R386	Speaker Arg1:T285 Arg2:T288	
T290	Quotation 57363 57387	"My poor dear Isabella,"
T291	Cue 57388 57392	said
T292	Entity 57393 57395	he
R387	Cueing Arg1:T291 Arg2:T290	
R388	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T290	
T293	Entity 57411 57414	her
R389	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T290	
T294	Quotation 57511 57808	"How long it is, how terribly long since you were here! And how tired you must be after your journey!  You must go to bed early, my dear-- and I recommend a little gruel to you before you go.-- You and I will have a nice basin of gruel together.  My dear Emma, suppose we all have a little gruel."
R390	Cueing Arg1:T291 Arg2:T294	
R391	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T294	
R392	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T294	
T295	Entity 57809 57813	Emma
R393	Addressee Arg1:T295 Arg2:T294	
T296	Quotation 58160 58302	"It was an awkward business, my dear, your spending the autumn at South End instead of coming here.  I never had much opinion of the sea air."
R394	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T296	
R395	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T296	
T297	Quotation 58303 58514	"Mr. Wingfield most strenuously recommended it, sir-- or we should not have gone.  He recommended it for all the children, but particularly for the weakness in little Bella's throat,-- both sea air and bathing."
R396	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T297	
R397	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T297	
T298	Quotation 58515 58773	"Ah! my dear, but Perry had many doubts about the sea doing her any good; and as to myself, I have been long perfectly convinced, though perhaps I never told you so before, that the sea is very rarely of use to any body.  I am sure it almost killed me once."
R398	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T298	
R399	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T298	
T299	Quotation 58774 58787	"Come, come,"
T300	Cue 58788 58793	cried
T301	Entity 58794 58798	Emma
T302	Quotation 58838 59088	"I must beg you not to talk of the sea.  It makes me envious and miserable; -- I who have never seen it!  South End is prohibited, if you please.  My dear Isabella, I have not heard you make one inquiry about Mr. Perry yet; and he never forgets you."
R400	Cueing Arg1:T300 Arg2:T299	
R401	Cueing Arg1:T300 Arg2:T302	
R402	Speaker Arg1:T301 Arg2:T299	
R403	Speaker Arg1:T301 Arg2:T302	
R404	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T299	
R405	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T299	
R406	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T302	
R407	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T302	
T303	Quotation 59089 59127	"Oh! good Mr. Perry-- how is he, sir?"
R408	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T303	
R409	Speaker Arg1:T293 Arg2:T303	
T304	Quotation 59128 59465	"Why, pretty well; but not quite well.  Poor Perry is bilious, and he has not time to take care of himself-- he tells me he has not time to take care of himself-- which is very sad-- but he is always wanted all round the country.  I suppose there is not a man in such practice anywhere.  But then there is not so clever a man any where."
R410	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T304	
R411	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T304	
T305	Quotation 59466 59651	"And Mrs. Perry and the children, how are they? do the children grow? I have a great regard for Mr. Perry.  I hope he will be calling soon. He will be so pleased to see my little ones."
R412	Speaker Arg1:T293 Arg2:T305	
R413	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T305	
T306	Quotation 59652 59849	"I hope he will be here to-morrow, for I have a question or two to ask him about myself of some consequence.  And, my dear, whenever he comes, you had better let him look at little Bella's throat."
R414	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T306	
R415	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T306	
T307	Quotation 59850 60133	"Oh! my dear sir, her throat is so much better that I have hardly any uneasiness about it.  Either bathing has been of the greatest service to her, or else it is to be attributed to an excellent embrocation of Mr. Wingfield's, which we have been applying at times ever since August."
R416	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T307	
R417	Speaker Arg1:T293 Arg2:T307	
T308	Quotation 60134 60345	"It is not very likely, my dear, that bathing should have been of use to her-- and if I had known you were wanting an embrocation, I would have spoken to--  You seem to me to have forgotten Mrs. and Miss Bates,"
T309	Cue 60346 60350	said
T310	Entity 60351 60355	Emma
T311	Quotation 60357 60399	"I have not heard one inquiry after them."
R418	Cueing Arg1:T309 Arg2:T308	
R419	Cueing Arg1:T309 Arg2:T311	
R420	Speaker Arg1:T310 Arg2:T308	
R421	Speaker Arg1:T310 Arg2:T311	
R422	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T311	
R423	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T308	
T312	Quotation 60400 60743	"Oh! the good Bateses -- I am quite ashamed of myself-- but you mention them in most of your letters.  I hope they are quite well.  Good old Mrs. Bates -- I will call upon her to-morrow, and take my children.-- They are always so pleased to see my children.-- And that excellent Miss Bates!-- such thorough worthy people!-- How are they, sir?"
R424	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T312	
R425	Speaker Arg1:T293 Arg2:T312	
T313	Quotation 60744 60843	"Why, pretty well, my dear, upon the whole.  But poor Mrs. Bates had a bad cold about a month ago."
R426	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T313	
R427	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T313	
T314	Quotation 60844 61047	"How sorry I am!  But colds were never so prevalent as they have been this autumn.  Mr. Wingfield told me that he has never known them more general or heavy-- except when it has been quite an influenza."
R428	Speaker Arg1:T293 Arg2:T314	
R429	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T314	
T315	Quotation 61048 61290	"That has been a good deal the case, my dear; but not to the degree you mention.  Perry says that colds have been very general, but not so heavy as he has very often known them in November.  Perry does not call it altogether a sickly season."
T316	Quotation 61291 61584	"No, I do not know that Mr. Wingfield considers it very sickly except--  Ah! my poor dear child, the truth is, that in London it is always a sickly season.  Nobody is healthy in London, nobody can be.  It is a dreadful thing to have you forced to live there! so far off!-- and the air so bad!"
R430	Speaker Arg1:T310 Arg2:T315	
R431	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T316	
R432	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T315	
R433	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T316	
T317	Quotation 61585 62130	"No, indeed-- we are not at all in a bad air.  Our part of London is very superior to most others!-- You must not confound us with London in general, my dear sir.  The neighbourhood of Brunswick Square is very different from almost all the rest.  We are so very airy!  I should be unwilling, I own, to live in any other part of the town;-- there is hardly any other that I could be satisfied to have my children in:  but we are so remarkably airy!-- Mr. Wingfield thinks the vicinity of Brunswick Square decidedly the most favourable as to air."
R434	Speaker Arg1:T293 Arg2:T317	
R435	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T317	
T318	Quotation 62131 62390	"Ah! my dear, it is not like Hartfield.  You make the best of it-- but after you have been a week at Hartfield, you are all of you different creatures; you do not look like the same.  Now I cannot say, that I think you are any of you looking well at present."
R436	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T318	
R437	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T318	
T319	Quotation 62391 62991	"I am sorry to hear you say so, sir; but I assure you, excepting those little nervous head-aches and palpitations which I am never entirely free from anywhere, I am quite well myself; and if the children were rather pale before they went to bed, it was only because they were a little more tired than usual, from their journey and the happiness of coming.  I hope you will think better of their looks to-morrow; for I assure you Mr. Wingfield told me, that he did not believe he had ever sent us off altogether, in such good case.  I trust, at least, that you do not think Mr. Knightley looking ill,"
T320	Entity 63000 63003	her
R438	Speaker Arg1:T320 Arg2:T319	
R439	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T319	
T321	Quotation 63056 63157	"Middling, my dear; I cannot compliment you.  I think Mr. John Knightley very far from looking well."
R440	Addressee Arg1:T320 Arg2:T321	
R441	Speaker Arg1:T292 Arg2:T321	
T322	Quotation 63158 63207	"What is the matter, sir?-- Did you speak to me?"
T323	Cue 63208 63213	cried
T324	Entity 63214 63232	Mr. John Knightley
R442	Cueing Arg1:T323 Arg2:T322	
R443	Speaker Arg1:T324 Arg2:T322	
R444	Addressee Arg1:T292 Arg2:T322	
T325	Quotation 63256 63485	"I am sorry to find, my love, that my father does not think you looking well-- but I hope it is only from being a little fatigued.  I could have wished, however, as you know, that you had seen Mr. Wingfield before you left home."
R445	Addressee Arg1:T324 Arg2:T325	
R446	Speaker Arg1:T320 Arg2:T325	
T326	Quotation 63486 63505	"My dear Isabella,"
T327	Quotation 63531 63674	"pray do not concern yourself about my looks.  Be satisfied with doctoring and coddling yourself and the children, and let me look as I chuse."
T328	Cue 63508 63517	exclaimed
T329	Entity 63518 63520	he
R447	Cueing Arg1:T328 Arg2:T326	
R448	Cueing Arg1:T328 Arg2:T327	
