R450	Speaker Arg1:T329 Arg2:T327	
R451	Addressee Arg1:T320 Arg2:T326	
R452	Addressee Arg1:T320 Arg2:T327	
T330	Quotation 63675 63744	"I did not thoroughly understand what you were telling your brother,"
T331	Cue 63745 63750	cried
T332	Entity 63751 63755	Emma
T333	Quotation 63757 63926	"about your friend Mr. Graham's intending to have a bailiff from Scotland, to look after his new estate.  What will it answer? Will not the old prejudice be too strong?"
R453	Cueing Arg1:T331 Arg2:T330	
R454	Cueing Arg1:T331 Arg2:T333	
R455	Speaker Arg1:T332 Arg2:T330	
R456	Speaker Arg1:T332 Arg2:T333	
R457	Addressee Arg1:T329 Arg2:T330	
R458	Addressee Arg1:T329 Arg2:T333	
T334	Quotation 64252 64287	"That sweet, amiable Jane Fairfax!"
T335	Cue 64288 64292	said
T336	Entity 64293 64312	Mrs. John Knightley
T337	Quotation 64316 64768	"It is so long since I have seen her, except now and then for a moment accidentally in town!  What happiness it must be to her good old grandmother and excellent aunt, when she comes to visit them!  I always regret excessively on dear Emma's account that she cannot be more at Highbury; but now their daughter is married, I suppose Colonel and Mrs. Campbell will not be able to part with her at all.  She would be such a delightful companion for Emma."
R459	Cueing Arg1:T335 Arg2:T334	
R460	Cueing Arg1:T335 Arg2:T337	
R461	Speaker Arg1:T336 Arg2:T334	
R462	Speaker Arg1:T336 Arg2:T337	
T338	Entity 64769 64782	Mr. Woodhouse
R463	Addressee Arg1:T338 Arg2:T334	
R464	Addressee Arg1:T338 Arg2:T337	
T339	Quotation 64812 64983	"Our little friend Harriet Smith, however, is just such another pretty kind of young person.  You will like Harriet.  Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet."
R465	Speaker Arg1:T338 Arg2:T339	
T340	Cue 64805 64810	added
R466	Cueing Arg1:T340 Arg2:T339	
R467	Addressee Arg1:T336 Arg2:T339	
T341	Quotation 64984 65113	"I am most happy to hear it-- but only Jane Fairfax one knows to be so very accomplished and superior!-- and exactly Emma's age."
R468	Addressee Arg1:T338 Arg2:T341	
R469	Speaker Arg1:T336 Arg2:T341	
T342	Quotation 65970 65975	"Ah!"
T343	Cue 65976 65980	said
T344	Entity 65981 65994	Mr. Woodhouse
T345	Entity 66036 66039	her
R470	Cueing Arg1:T343 Arg2:T342	
R471	Speaker Arg1:T344 Arg2:T342	
R472	Addressee Arg1:T345 Arg2:T342	
T346	Entity 66408 66410	he
T347	Cue 66411 66416	began
T348	Quotation 66423 66515	"I shall always be very sorry that you went to the sea this autumn, instead of coming here."
R473	Speaker Arg1:T346 Arg2:T348	
R474	Cueing Arg1:T347 Arg2:T348	
R475	Addressee Arg1:T345 Arg2:T348	
T349	Quotation 66516 66610	"But why should you be sorry, sir? -- I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good."
R476	Addressee Arg1:T346 Arg2:T349	
R477	Speaker Arg1:T345 Arg2:T349	
T350	Quotation 66611 66789	"And, moreover, if you must go to the sea, it had better not have been to South End.  South End is an unhealthy place.  Perry was surprized to hear you had fixed upon South End."
R478	Speaker Arg1:T346 Arg2:T350	
R479	Addressee Arg1:T345 Arg2:T350	
T351	Quotation 66790 67202	"I know there is such an idea with many people, but indeed it is quite a mistake, sir.-- We all had our health perfectly well there, never found the least inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely a mistake to suppose the place unhealthy; and I am sure he may be depended on, for he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have been there repeatedly."
R480	Addressee Arg1:T346 Arg2:T351	
R481	Speaker Arg1:T345 Arg2:T351	
T352	Quotation 67203 67577	"You should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere.-- Perry was a week at Cromer once, and he holds it to be the best of all the sea-bathing places.  A fine open sea, he says, and very pure air.  And, by what I understand, you might have had lodgings there quite away from the sea -- a quarter of a mile off-- very comfortable.  You should have consulted Perry."
R482	Speaker Arg1:T346 Arg2:T352	
R483	Addressee Arg1:T345 Arg2:T352	
T353	Quotation 67578 67721	"But, my dear sir, the difference of the journey;-- only consider how great it would have been.-- An hundred miles, perhaps, instead of forty."
R484	Addressee Arg1:T346 Arg2:T353	
R485	Speaker Arg1:T345 Arg2:T353	
T354	Quotation 67722 68084	"Ah! my dear, as Perry says, where health is at stake, nothing else should be considered; and if one is to travel, there is not much to chuse between forty miles and an hundred.-- Better not move at all, better stay in London altogether than travel forty miles to get into a worse air.  This is just what Perry said.  It seemed to him a very ill-judged measure."
R486	Speaker Arg1:T346 Arg2:T354	
R487	Addressee Arg1:T345 Arg2:T354	
T355	Quotation 68240 68252	"Mr. Perry,"
T356	Cue 68253 68257	said
T357	Entity 68258 68260	he
T358	Quotation 68301 68611	"would do as well to keep his opinion till it is asked for.  Why does he make it any business of his, to wonder at what I do?-- at my taking my family to one part of the coast or another? -- I may be allowed, I hope, the use of my judgment as well as Mr. Perry.-- I want his directions no more than his drugs."
R488	Cueing Arg1:T356 Arg2:T355	
R489	Cueing Arg1:T356 Arg2:T358	
R490	Speaker Arg1:T357 Arg2:T355	
R491	Speaker Arg1:T357 Arg2:T358	
T359	Entity 68109 68119	her father
R492	Addressee Arg1:T359 Arg2:T355	
R493	Addressee Arg1:T359 Arg2:T358	
T360	Quotation 68692 68940	"If Mr. Perry can tell me how to convey a wife and five children a distance of an hundred and thirty miles with no greater expense or inconvenience than a distance of forty, I should be as willing to prefer Cromer to South End as he could himself."
T361	Cue 68656 68661	added
R494	Cueing Arg1:T361 Arg2:T360	
T362	Entity 68612 68614	He
R495	Speaker Arg1:T362 Arg2:T360	
R496	Addressee Arg1:T359 Arg2:T360	
T363	Quotation 68941 68954	"True, true,"
T364	Cue 68955 68960	cried
T365	Entity 68961 68974	Mr. Knightley
T366	Quotation 69008 69601	"very true.  That's a consideration indeed.-- But John, as to what I was telling you of my idea of moving the path to Langham, of turning it more to the right that it may not cut through the home meadows, I cannot conceive any difficulty.  I should not attempt it, if it were to be the means of inconvenience to the Highbury people, but if you call to mind exactly the present line of the path. . . .  The only way of proving it, however, will be to turn to our maps.  I shall see you at the Abbey to-morrow morning I hope, and then we will look them over, and you shall give me your opinion."
R497	Cueing Arg1:T364 Arg2:T363	
R498	Cueing Arg1:T364 Arg2:T366	
R499	Speaker Arg1:T365 Arg2:T363	
R500	Speaker Arg1:T365 Arg2:T366	
R501	Addressee Arg1:T362 Arg2:T363	
R502	Addressee Arg1:T362 Arg2:T366	
T367	Entity 73112 73116	Emma
T368	Quotation 73177 73400	"a throat very much inflamed, with a great deal of heat about her, a quick, low pulse, &c.  and she was sorry to find from Mrs. Goddard that Harriet was liable to very bad sore-throats, and had often alarmed her with them."
T369	Entity 73067 73071	They
R503	Addressee Arg1:T369 Arg2:T368	
R504	Speaker Arg1:T367 Arg2:T368	
T370	Entity 73401 73410	Mr. Elton
T371	Cue 73451 73460	exclaimed
T372	Quotation 73462 73700	"A sore-throat! -- I hope not infectious.  I hope not of a putrid infectious sort.  Has Perry seen her?  Indeed you should take care of yourself as well as of your friend.  Let me entreat you to run no risks.  Why does not Perry see her?"
R505	Cueing Arg1:T371 Arg2:T372	
R506	Speaker Arg1:T370 Arg2:T372	
R507	Addressee Arg1:T367 Arg2:T372	
T373	Quotation 74049 74748	"It is so cold, so very cold-- and looks and feels so very much like snow, that if it were to any other place or with any other party, I should really try not to go out to-day-- and dissuade my father from venturing; but as he has made up his mind, and does not seem to feel the cold himself, I do not like to interfere, as I know it would be so great a disappointment to Mr. and Mrs. Weston.  But, upon my word, Mr. Elton, in your case, I should certainly excuse myself.  You appear to me a little hoarse already, and when you consider what demand of voice and what fatigues to-morrow will bring, I think it would be no more than common prudence to stay at home and take care of yourself to-night."
T374	Entity 73701 73705	Emma
T375	Entity 74749 74758	Mr. Elton
