T394	Quotation 77999 78004	"Me!"
T395	Entity 78005 78008	she
T396	Cue 78009 78016	replied
T397	Quotation 78047 78095	"are you imagining me to be Mr. Elton's object?"
R533	Speaker Arg1:T395 Arg2:T394	
R534	Speaker Arg1:T395 Arg2:T397	
R535	Cueing Arg1:T396 Arg2:T394	
R536	Cueing Arg1:T396 Arg2:T397	
R537	Addressee Arg1:T390 Arg2:T394	
R538	Addressee Arg1:T390 Arg2:T397	
T398	Quotation 78096 78234	"Such an imagination has crossed me, I own, Emma; and if it never occurred to you before, you may as well take it into consideration now."
R539	Addressee Arg1:T395 Arg2:T398	
R540	Speaker Arg1:T390 Arg2:T398	
T399	Quotation 78235 78279	"Mr. Elton in love with me!-- What an idea!"
R541	Speaker Arg1:T395 Arg2:T399	
R542	Addressee Arg1:T390 Arg2:T399	
T400	Quotation 78280 78560	"I do not say it is so; but you will do well to consider whether it is so or not, and to regulate your behaviour accordingly.  I think your manners to him encouraging.  I speak as a friend, Emma.  You had better look about you, and ascertain what you do, and what you mean to do."
R543	Addressee Arg1:T395 Arg2:T400	
R544	Speaker Arg1:T390 Arg2:T400	
T401	Quotation 78561 78674	"I thank you; but I assure you you are quite mistaken.  Mr. Elton and I are very good friends, and nothing more;"
T402	Entity 78679 78682	she
T403	Entity 78927 78938	her brother
R545	Speaker Arg1:T402 Arg2:T401	
R546	Addressee Arg1:T403 Arg2:T401	
T404	Quotation 80177 80185	"A man,"
T405	Cue 80186 80190	said
T406	Entity 80191 80193	he
T407	Quotation 80195 81457	"must have a very good opinion of himself when he asks people to leave their own fireside, and encounter such a day as this, for the sake of coming to see him.  He must think himself a most agreeable fellow; I could not do such a thing.  It is the greatest absurdity-- Actually snowing at this moment!-- The folly of not allowing people to be comfortable at home-- and the folly of people's not staying comfortably at home when they can!  If we were obliged to go out such an evening as this, by any call of duty or business, what a hardship we should deem it;-- and here are we, probably with rather thinner clothing than usual, setting forward voluntarily, without excuse, in defiance of the voice of nature, which tells man, in every thing given to his view or his feelings, to stay at home himself, and keep all under shelter that he can;-- here are we setting forward to spend five dull hours in another man's house, with nothing to say or to hear that was not said and heard yesterday, and may not be said and heard again to-morrow. Going in dismal weather, to return probably in worse;-- four horses and four servants taken out for nothing but to convey five idle, shivering creatures into colder rooms and worse company than they might have had at home."
R547	Cueing Arg1:T405 Arg2:T404	
R548	Speaker Arg1:T406 Arg2:T404	
R549	Cueing Arg1:T405 Arg2:T407	
R550	Speaker Arg1:T406 Arg2:T407	
T408	Entity 81458 81462	Emma
R551	Addressee Arg1:T408 Arg2:T404	
T409	Quotation 82414 82446	"My report from Mrs. Goddard's,"
T410	Cue 82447 82451	said
T411	Entity 82452 82455	she
T412	Quotation 82467 82533	"was not so pleasant as I had hoped-- 'Not better' was my answer."
R552	Cueing Arg1:T410 Arg2:T409	
R553	Cueing Arg1:T410 Arg2:T412	
R554	Speaker Arg1:T411 Arg2:T409	
R555	Speaker Arg1:T411 Arg2:T412	
T413	Entity 82073 82077	Emma
R556	Addressee Arg1:T413 Arg2:T409	
R557	Addressee Arg1:T413 Arg2:T412	
T414	Quotation 82624 83021	"Oh! no -- I am grieved to find -- I was on the point of telling you that when I called at Mrs. Goddard's door, which I did the very last thing before I returned to dress, I was told that Miss Smith was not better, by no means better, rather worse.  Very much grieved and concerned-- I had flattered myself that she must be better after such a cordial as I knew had been given her in the morning."
T415	Entity 82611 82613	he
R558	Speaker Arg1:T415 Arg2:T414	
T416	Cue 82614 82622	answered
R559	Cueing Arg1:T416 Arg2:T414	
T417	Entity 83022 83026	Emma
R560	Addressee Arg1:T417 Arg2:T414	
T418	Cue 83038 83046	answered
T419	Quotation 83049 83247	"My visit was of use to the nervous part of her complaint, I hope; but not even I can charm away a sore throat; it is a most severe cold indeed.  Mr. Perry has been with her, as you probably heard."
R561	Speaker Arg1:T417 Arg2:T419	
R562	Cueing Arg1:T418 Arg2:T419	
R563	Addressee Arg1:T415 Arg2:T419	
T420	Quotation 83248 83294	"Yes -- I imagined-- that is -- I did not -- "
R564	Addressee Arg1:T417 Arg2:T420	
R565	Speaker Arg1:T415 Arg2:T420	
T421	Quotation 83295 83500	"He has been used to her in these complaints, and I hope to-morrow morning will bring us both a more comfortable report.  But it is impossible not to feel uneasiness.  Such a sad loss to our party to-day!"
R566	Speaker Arg1:T417 Arg2:T421	
R567	Addressee Arg1:T415 Arg2:T421	
T422	Quotation 83501 83569	"Dreadful!-- Exactly so, indeed.-- She will be missed every moment."
R568	Addressee Arg1:T417 Arg2:T422	
R569	Speaker Arg1:T415 Arg2:T422	
T423	Quotation 83830 83857	"What an excellent device,"
T424	Cue 83858 83862	said
T425	Entity 83863 83865	he
T426	Quotation 83867 84349	"the use of a sheepskin for carriages.  How very comfortable they make it;-- impossible to feel cold with such precautions.  The contrivances of modern days indeed have rendered a gentleman's carriage perfectly complete.  One is so fenced and guarded from the weather, that not a breath of air can find its way unpermitted.  Weather becomes absolutely of no consequence.  It is a very cold afternoon-- but in this carriage we know nothing of the matter.-- Ha! snows a little I see."
R570	Cueing Arg1:T424 Arg2:T423	
R571	Cueing Arg1:T424 Arg2:T426	
R572	Speaker Arg1:T425 Arg2:T423	
R573	Speaker Arg1:T425 Arg2:T426	
T427	Quotation 84350 84356	"Yes,"
T428	Cue 84357 84361	said
T429	Entity 84362 84376	John Knightley
T430	Quotation 84378 84424	"and I think we shall have a good deal of it."
R574	Cueing Arg1:T428 Arg2:T427	
R575	Cueing Arg1:T428 Arg2:T430	
R576	Speaker Arg1:T429 Arg2:T427	
R577	Speaker Arg1:T429 Arg2:T430	
T431	Quotation 84425 84445	"Christmas weather,"
T432	Cue 84446 84454	observed
T433	Entity 84455 84464	Mr. Elton
R578	Cueing Arg1:T432 Arg2:T431	
R579	Speaker Arg1:T433 Arg2:T431	
R580	Addressee Arg1:T429 Arg2:T431	
T434	Quotation 84467 85084	"Quite seasonable; and extremely fortunate we may think ourselves that it did not begin yesterday, and prevent this day's party, which it might very possibly have done, for Mr. Woodhouse would hardly have ventured had there been much snow on the ground; but now it is of no consequence.  This is quite the season indeed for friendly meetings.  At Christmas every body invites their friends about them, and people think little of even the worst weather.  I was snowed up at a friend's house once for a week. Nothing could be pleasanter.  I went for only one night, and could not get away till that very day se'nnight."
R581	Cueing Arg1:T432 Arg2:T434	
R582	Speaker Arg1:T433 Arg2:T434	
R583	Addressee Arg1:T429 Arg2:T434	
T435	Entity 85085 85103	Mr. John Knightley
T436	Cue 85157 85161	said
T437	Quotation 85176 85227	"I cannot wish to be snowed up a week at Randalls."
R584	Cueing Arg1:T436 Arg2:T437	
R585	Speaker Arg1:T435 Arg2:T437	
R586	Addressee Arg1:T433 Arg2:T437	
T438	Quotation 85424 85457	"We are sure of excellent fires,"
T439	Cue 85458 85467	continued
T440	Quotation 85472 86184	"and every thing in the greatest comfort.  Charming people, Mr. and Mrs. Weston;-- Mrs. Weston indeed is much beyond praise, and he is exactly what one values, so hospitable, and so fond of society;-- it will be a small party, but where small parties are select, they are perhaps the most agreeable of any.  Mr. Weston's dining-room does not accommodate more than ten comfortably; and for my part, I would rather, under such circumstances, fall short by two than exceed by two.  I think you will agree with me, (turning with a soft air to Emma,) I think I shall certainly have your approbation, though Mr. Knightley perhaps, from being used to the large parties of London, may not quite enter into our feelings."
T441	Entity 85468 85470	he
R587	Cueing Arg1:T439 Arg2:T438	
R588	Speaker Arg1:T441 Arg2:T438	
R589	Cueing Arg1:T439 Arg2:T440	
R590	Speaker Arg1:T441 Arg2:T440	
R591	Addressee Arg1:T435 Arg2:T438	
R592	Addressee Arg1:T435 Arg2:T440	
T442	Quotation 86185 86268	"I know nothing of the large parties of London, sir -- I never dine with any body."
R593	Addressee Arg1:T441 Arg2:T442	
R594	Speaker Arg1:T435 Arg2:T442	
T443	Quotation 86269 86487	"Indeed! (in a tone of wonder and pity,) I had no idea that the law had been so great a slavery.  Well, sir, the time must come when you will be paid for all this, when you will have little labour and great enjoyment."
R595	Speaker Arg1:T441 Arg2:T443	
T444	Quotation 86488 86509	"My first enjoyment,"
T445	Cue 86510 86517	replied
T446	Entity 86518 86532	John Knightley
T447	Quotation 86573 86622	"will be to find myself safe at Hartfield again."
R596	Cueing Arg1:T445 Arg2:T444	
R597	Cueing Arg1:T445 Arg2:T447	
