T754	Quotation 182360 182403	"I hope every body had a pleasant evening,"
T755	Cue 182404 182408	said
T756	Entity 182409 182422	Mr. Woodhouse
R977	Cueing Arg1:T755 Arg2:T754	
R978	Speaker Arg1:T756 Arg2:T754	
R979	Addressee Arg1:T752 Arg2:T754	
R980	Addressee Arg1:T748 Arg2:T754	
T757	Quotation 182443 182965	"I had.  Once, I felt the fire rather too much; but then I moved back my chair a little, a very little, and it did not disturb me. Miss Bates was very chatty and good-humoured, as she always is, though she speaks rather too quick.  However, she is very agreeable, and Mrs. Bates too, in a different way.  I like old friends; and Miss Jane Fairfax is a very pretty sort of young lady, a very pretty and a very well-behaved young lady indeed.  She must have found the evening agreeable, Mr. Knightley, because she had Emma."
R981	Speaker Arg1:T756 Arg2:T757	
R982	Cueing Arg1:T755 Arg2:T757	
R983	Addressee Arg1:T752 Arg2:T757	
R984	Addressee Arg1:T748 Arg2:T757	
T758	Quotation 182966 183018	"True, sir; and Emma, because she had Miss Fairfax."
R985	Addressee Arg1:T756 Arg2:T758	
R986	Speaker Arg1:T752 Arg2:T758	
T759	Quotation 183151 183294	"She is a sort of elegant creature that one cannot keep one's eyes from.  I am always watching her to admire; and I do pity her from my heart."
T760	Entity 183019 183023	Emma
T761	Entity 183028 183031	his
T762	Cue 183094 183098	said
R987	Speaker Arg1:T760 Arg2:T759	
R988	Addressee Arg1:T761 Arg2:T759	
R989	Cueing Arg1:T762 Arg2:T759	
T763	Entity 183407 183420	Mr. Woodhouse
T764	Cue 183458 183462	said
T765	Quotation 183465 184113	"It is a great pity that their circumstances should be so confined!  a great pity indeed! and I have often wished-- but it is so little one can venture to do-- small, trifling presents, of any thing uncommon-- Now we have killed a porker, and Emma thinks of sending them a loin or a leg; it is very small and delicate-- Hartfield pork is not like any other pork-- but still it is pork-- and, my dear Emma, unless one could be sure of their making it into steaks, nicely fried, as ours are fried, without the smallest grease, and not roast it, for no stomach can bear roast pork -- I think we had better send the leg-- do not you think so, my dear?"
R990	Cueing Arg1:T764 Arg2:T765	
R991	Speaker Arg1:T763 Arg2:T765	
R992	Addressee Arg1:T760 Arg2:T765	
T766	Quotation 184114 184316	"My dear papa, I sent the whole hind-quarter. I knew you would wish it. There will be the leg to be salted, you know, which is so very nice, and the loin to be dressed directly in any manner they like."
R993	Speaker Arg1:T760 Arg2:T766	
R994	Addressee Arg1:T763 Arg2:T766	
T767	Quotation 184317 184663	"That's right, my dear, very right.  I had not thought of it before, but that is the best way.  They must not over-salt the leg; and then, if it is not over-salted, and if it is very thoroughly boiled, just as Serle boils ours, and eaten very moderately of, with a boiled turnip, and a little carrot or parsnip, I do not consider it unwholesome."
R995	Speaker Arg1:T763 Arg2:T767	
R996	Addressee Arg1:T760 Arg2:T767	
